# Independent Windows WebKit focus isolation

Result: the observed failure is reproduced without production HTML or CSS. It is consistent with WebKit's platform-specific link-tab preference, rather than a defect introduced by the generated Doc fragment. This is not a Windows WebKit keyboard-accessibility PASS.

The independent `probe.py` ran 18 fresh contexts: WebKit, Chromium, Firefox; plain links, plain links plus form controls, frozen generated chapter 13; JavaScript enabled and disabled. The local server served only scratch copies. Each context recorded six Tab presses, then reloaded and recorded three Alt+Tab presses. Diagnostic keyboard/focus listeners did not cancel events or change focus. No tabindex attributes were added. The explicit `locator.focus()` happened only afterward and is not counted as keyboard navigation.

- Windows WebKit 26.5 leaves BODY active for all six Tab presses on the plain two-link control and production document, with either JavaScript setting. Alt+Tab after reload also leaves BODY active.
- On the plain form control, WebKit Tab visits INPUT, BUTTON, BODY, INPUT, BUTTON, BODY and skips both ordinary anchors. Thus the keyboard events are delivered and sequential focus works for form controls.
- Chromium 151.0.7922.34 and Firefox 153.0 reach the first two anchors with the first two Tab presses on both plain links and production, with either JavaScript setting. This finding does not claim identical later browser-chrome focus cycling.
- All inspected anchors have DOM tabIndex 0 and no explicit tabindex attribute. Programmatic focus reaches an anchor in all contexts; this only distinguishes intrinsic focusability from sequential navigation policy.

`results.json` preserves actual focus states and keyboard events, versions, and executable-path hashes. Python is 3.13.12; Playwright is 1.62.0; Windows reports 11/10.0.26200. The hashes are of `browser_type.executable_path`, not a claim to hash every browser component or identify the actual Chromium headless-shell child process.

## Official implementation evidence

The pinned [Playwright v1.62.0 upstream configuration](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.62.0/browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh) identifies WebKit base revision `343e13bf22dca9d0ec227801419aab0f9001a32f`. Raw copies are retained.

At that revision, [UnifiedWebPreferences.yaml](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/343e13bf22dca9d0ec227801419aab0f9001a32f/Source/WTF/Scripts/Preferences/UnifiedWebPreferences.yaml#L8060) defines TabsToLinks as an embedder preference: GTK/WPE true, other WebKit platforms false. [WebPage.cpp](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/343e13bf22dca9d0ec227801419aab0f9001a32f/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/WebPage.cpp#L4631) incorporates the preference into KeyboardUIMode and loads it from the preference store at line 5008.

[EventHandler.cpp](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/343e13bf22dca9d0ec227801419aab0f9001a32f/Source/WebCore/page/EventHandler.cpp#L4957) enables Option+Tab inversion only for PLATFORM(COCOA), returns false elsewhere, and permits tab navigation to all form controls on non-Cocoa platforms. This explains why applying the macOS Option/Alt advice on this Windows harness does not solve the control failure. The inspected Playwright Windows embedder constructor creates WKView and marks its page as controlled by automation; it does not set TabsToLinks.

Inference boundary: the native preference value was not queried in the installed binary, and the complete Playwright patch stack was not proven identical to the raw upstream files. The exact Windows behavior is directly measured; its correspondence with these pinned source conditions is strong supporting evidence. Do not report a successfully changed preference or a passed Windows WebKit keyboard test.

## Scope and next action

Preserve the Windows WebKit link-tab failure as an environment-specific observation. Do not modify production anchors with tabindex to satisfy this probe. If the acceptance target is Linux CI WebKit, execute its actual lane separately: GTK/WPE's documented default differs, and this Windows result cannot establish that lane's result. This review did not launch Safari/macOS, headed browsers, visual screenshot QA, real assistive technology, or change production/CI/browser settings. It establishes no complete accessibility guarantee or canonical cutover.

The generated document and CSS used here were copied byte-for-byte before execution. The fixture files and all direct probe outputs are enumerated in `manifest.json`; `summary.json` is a checked reduction of the raw result, not a separate browser run.
